on site"}, {"name": "New York City, USA", "role": "Exhibition site"}, {"name": "Paris, France", "role": "Cultural influence"}, {"name": "London, England", "role": "Art market hub"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Artist's Shit", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "artists-shit"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1757822", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artist%27s_Shit"], "tags": ["work of art", "Medical Institutions & Hospitals", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Artist's Shit contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Artist's Shit significantly impacted the discourse around the definition and value of art, influencing numerous art movements and discussions about the commodification of art.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-03T20:56:45Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28682827364", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "1961 artwork by piero manzoni consisting of 90 cans filled with his faeces Founded 1961.", "newValue": "Artist's Shit is a provocative artwork created by Italian artist Piero Manzoni in 1961, consisting of 90 tin cans purportedly containing his own feces. This work emerged from the broader context of the contemporary art movement, particularly Dada and conceptual art, which sought to challenge traditional notions of art and the artist's role in society. By presenting excrement as art, Manzoni not only questioned the commodification of art but also the very definition of what constitutes art itself. This bold statement resonates globally, as it reflects a significant shift in artistic expression during the 20th century, making it a landmark in the history of contemporary art.

The creation of Artist's Shit coincided with a period marked by radical experimentation in the visual arts, notably in Europe and North America. The cans, each labeled with the title and a unique number, were sold at a price equivalent to gold at the time, underscoring the irony of valuing something so base and organic. Manzoni's work acts as a critique of the art market and the commercialization of artistic talent, serving as a metaphor for the absurdity of placing monetary value on art. His actions were both a rebellion against and a reflection of the avant-garde movements that characterized the 1960s.

